import json
A = {}
A["stage1/problem/A1/v2"] = """Problem: How much of the zero-shot localisation quality of MVFA (pixel AUROC and Dice on a held-out modality) is retained when its segmentation adapters are trained without pixel masks, using pseudo-masks drawn only from labelled abnormal source images (a patch is anomalous when its frozen CLIP level-4 feature is farther from every normal patch of the same dataset than the 99th percentile of normal-to-normal nearest-neighbour distances on source validation data), as masks are removed from zero, one, or all three masked source datasets?

Rationale: MVFA's anomaly maps come only from segmentation adapters trained with Dice and Focal losses on masks of BrainMRI, LiverCT and RESC (G3), so the method cannot learn localisation from image-level labelled archives, and the value of dense expert masks is unknown. Unlike CoDeGraph [30], which builds pseudo-masks from unlabelled batches and must filter consistent anomalies, image-level labels here restrict pseudo-masks to images known to be abnormal and define them against normal images of the same dataset, with a threshold fixed on source data only (its percentile is reported with a small sensitivity check). DDAD [54], MediCLIP [10] and SpatioPrompt [20] use unlabelled or synthetic signals in other frameworks; none tests MVFA's multi-level adapters. The primary result is a label-cost curve on the three masked datasets, preceded by a pseudo-mask quality check against true masks so that a negative result can be attributed; adding the image-only datasets (OCT17, ChestXray, HIS) as pseudo-mask sources is a secondary analysis. One loss target of one branch changes, pseudo-masks are computed once, and training cost equals MVFA's under the shared protocol."""

A["stage1/problem/A2/v2"] = """Problem: When MVFA is adapted from K normal support images only, with synthesised lesions following MediCLIP's recipe, does applying the synthetic supervision to the segmentation adapters, the detection adapters, or both retain the most of the image- and pixel-level AUROC of the standard K-normal + K-masked-abnormal protocol, and what is the smallest K at which the best normal-only variant matches the K+K protocol (K in {2, 4, 8, 16}, three support draws)?

Rationale: The released code defines 'K-shot' as K normal plus K abnormal images with masks (G4), so MVFA's few-shot results assume abnormal annotations that a clinic adopting a new modality rarely has, and the segmentation branch has no other route to supervision (G3). MediCLIP [10] and SpatioPrompt [20] show normal-only adaptation of CLIP with synthetic anomalies in single-branch designs; MVFA's separate detection and segmentation adapters raise a question those papers do not answer: which branch benefits from synthetic supervision, given that synthetic lesions transfer imperfectly to real pathology. The synthesis recipe is fixed, so the branch choice is the only variable. The smallest K at which normal-only adaptation matches K+K is the actionable number for deployment, and three support draws also answer the single-draw part of G4. Each few-shot run takes minutes on one GPU."""

A["stage1/problem/B1/v2"] = """Problem: Can MVFA's few-shot inference be made inductive, scoring each image only with statistics of the K normal support images and source-validation data instead of test-set min-max normalisation, while keeping image AUROC within 1 point of the re-run transductive baseline and making each image's score invariant to the composition of the rest of the test set (prevalence 10-50%, with and without outliers)? The replacement is evaluated component by component (normalisation, then fusion weight, then image-level aggregation), with sensitivity at 95% specificity and expected calibration error reported alongside AUROC.

Rationale: The released code min-max normalises each branch over the entire test set before fusing with weight 0.5 (G8), takes the image label from the maximum of the fused map (G9), and reports only AUC (G11). A deployed detector scores one image at a time, so the published numbers are not those of a usable system, and the size of the gap has not been measured. Inductive normalisation with validation statistics is common in anomaly-detection tooling and CANOR [55] calibrates CLIP scores with a few scalars, but no medical CLIP detector in the review set (LaRP-CLIP [17], HeadCLIP [4]) measures how transductive normalisation affects reported results. The headline measurement, the shift of an individual image's score when the rest of the test set is resampled, is a new diagnostic for this family of methods, and the transductive baseline's own shift is its reference. The study is inference-only on retrained checkpoints and takes hours on one GPU."""

A["stage1/problem/B2/v2"] = """Problem: Does the best image-level pooling of MVFA's anomaly maps depend on lesion size? Comparing max (paper, Eq. 3), mean (released training code), top-k mean with k set to the median lesion area of the masked source datasets, and noisy-OR, each applied identically in training and testing, which gives the highest image AUROC per held-out modality and per lesion-size tertile, and how does each degrade when spurious high-scoring pixels mimicking bright vessels or acquisition noise are injected into normal test maps?

Rationale: The paper and the released code disagree on pooling (G17), and the test-time image score on masked datasets is the maximum of the fused map, so a single spurious pixel can decide the label (G9); robustness is not measured (G11). CANOR [55] shows that noisy-OR improves on naive max/mean aggregation for CLIP chest X-ray scores, and LaRP-CLIP [17] and HeadCLIP [4] build image scores from pixel evidence, but none relates the choice to lesion size, which varies by an order of magnitude across BMAD. The hypothesis is that max suits small focal lesions and top-k or noisy-OR suit large or diffuse ones; k is fixed from the masked sources and reused for the image-only datasets. Each variant is a one-line change in training and testing, so four retrainings suffice."""

A["stage1/problem/D1/v2"] = """Problem: Are MVFA's adapted patch features compact enough that a per-level Gaussian with shrinkage covariance, or 32 k-means prototypes, fitted on the K normal support images can replace the few-shot memory bank without losing more than 1 point of pixel or image AUROC, and how do memory, latency and stored patient information scale with K (2-64) and input resolution (240 and 448) for each option, in a scenario where a site accumulates normal studies over time?

Rationale: MVFA compares query patches with a memory bank that grows with K and resolution and stores raw patient-derived features (G13), without modelling the compact normal distribution explicitly (G7). PaDiM-style Gaussians and Spatial-FAD's compact prototypes [6] work for other features, and AVA-DINO [14] argues normals are compact while anomalies are diverse; whether MVFA's adapted features share this property is untested. The study first tests per-level Gaussianity of adapted versus raw CLIP features (Mardia's multivariate skewness and kurtosis on a random projection), then compares the three options. The extended K and resolution model a site that keeps adding normal studies, where a growing raw-feature bank becomes a storage and governance burden. No retraining is needed."""

A["stage1/problem/D2/v2"] = """Problem: Do prompts learned on source modalities transfer to an unseen modality inside MVFA? With MVFA's visual adapters frozen at their trained checkpoint, learnable normal/abnormal prompt tokens (16 per state, optionally conditioned on the mean adapted patch feature) are trained on the source modalities and compared with the fixed 35-template prompts on the held-out modality, measuring zero-shot AUROC and the spread of AUROC across ten alternative wordings (five clinical, five generic).

Rationale: MVFA assumes a single universal text notion of normal and abnormal across modalities and never analyses prompt sensitivity (G5). Learnable and conditioned prompts are common in the review set (AnomalyCLIP [11], AdaCLIP [32], CoPS [41], VCF-CLIP [22], IQE-CLIP [7]) and medical prompt studies report instability across wordings [50], but none isolates cross-modality transfer of learned prompts under a modality-disjoint split, the setting MVFA claims. Freezing the visual adapters isolates the text side and keeps training cheap. The main outcome is reduced wording sensitivity; AUROC on held-out modalities is secondary."""
json.dump(A, open("logs/answers/run2/s1_v2.json", "w"), indent=1)
